WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.700
Farm safety net programs were
established to protect agricultural

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.290
producers against production, price, and
income risks. But payments for major

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:17.369
federal programs like agricultural risk
coverage, price loss coverage, and crop

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:22.080
insurance overwhelmingly go to the
wealthiest and largest farms. The top 10%

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.820
of farms by total crop sales, which have
an average net worth of 5.5 million,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.170
received $55,000 in payments per farm in
2015,

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.980
totaling almost $3 billion, or nearly
two-thirds of all program payments. The

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:39.480
other 90% of farms received just four
thousand dollars on average. Two recent

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.590
proposals attempt to limit the
concentration in subsidies. A $40,000 cap

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:47.190
on crop insurance subsidies would save
an estimated two billion dollars per

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.400
year, while $125,000 cap on per-farm subsidies across all programs, would

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.000
save $670 million per year. Nearly all of
these savings come from a reduction in

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.870
payments to the largest 5% of farms, who
are the least vulnerable to adverse

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.530
market conditions. In order for these
programs to provide an effective safety

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.740
net, they need to be targeted at the most
vulnerable.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:10.060
So what do you think the farm

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.650
safety net program should look like in
the United States? Let us know in your

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.369
comments. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:21.210
in 60 seconds, and be sure to LIKE and
subscribe for more research and videos

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.689
from AEI.

